<section class="tight">
  <div class="wrap wrap-narrow">
    <span class="eyebrow">Tutorial</span>
    <h1 style="font-size:clamp(2rem,4.4vw,3.1rem)">Build a guided chatbot in four files</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A two-stage assistant: it collects a city, then reports the weather. Small enough to
      read in one sitting, but it exercises the whole machine — persisted state, a validated
      tool, waiting for the user, advancing a stage, and finishing. By the end you will have
      run it and tested it <strong>without a model provider key</strong>.
    </p>

    <div class="callout tip">
      <span class="label">Source of truth</span>
      <p>
        Every snippet on this page is taken from
        <a href="/ezgraph/docs/developer-guide/"><b>the EZGraph developer's guide</b></a> —
        §3 <em>Setup</em>, §4 <em>Your first graph, end to end</em>, §5 <em>The turn
        lifecycle</em>, §15 <em>Testing</em>, and §17 <em>Pitfalls</em>. This page is the
        guided path through them; the guide is the complete reference, and it is shipped
        alongside this site so the two can never drift.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-3" style="margin:34px 0 10px">
      <div class="card" style="padding:20px">
        <h3 style="font-size:0.98rem;margin-bottom:6px">You'll need</h3>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem">Node ≥ 22.5, TypeScript 5.x, and about 15 minutes.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card" style="padding:20px">
        <h3 style="font-size:0.98rem;margin-bottom:6px">You won't need</h3>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem">An EZGraph account, a licence key, or a database — the
          default store is in-memory.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card" style="padding:20px">
        <h3 style="font-size:0.98rem;margin-bottom:6px">Model key</h3>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem">Needed only for step 6. Steps 1–5 and 7 run without one.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band tight">
  <div class="wrap wrap-narrow">
    <ol class="steps">

      <!-- ================= 1 ================= -->
      <li>
        <h3>Install and configure TypeScript</h3>
        <p>
          The EZGraph package, plus the adapter for whichever provider you use. Providers are
          optional peer dependencies — you install only what you call.
        </p>

        <div class="install" style="margin-bottom:12px">
          <span class="prompt">$</span>
          <code>npm install @picoflow/ezgraph</code>
        </div>
        <div class="install">
          <span class="prompt">$</span>
          <code>npm install @langchain/openai   # or @langchain/anthropic, @langchain/google</code>
        </div>

        <p style="margin-top:20px">
          Tool handlers use a method decorator, so decorator support and metadata emission
          are required. The framework is written under
          <code>exactOptionalPropertyTypes</code> and <code>noUncheckedIndexedAccess</code>;
          they aren't mandatory, but its types are most precise with them on.
        </p>

        <div class="code">
          <div class="code-head">
            <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
            <span class="file">tsconfig.json</span>
            <span class="note bad">required</span>
          </div>
<pre><code>{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "module": "NodeNext",
    "moduleResolution": "NodeNext",
    "target": "ES2022",
    "strict": true,
    "experimentalDecorators": true,
    "emitDecoratorMetadata": true,
    "exactOptionalPropertyTypes": true,
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true
  }
}</code></pre>
        </div>

        <div class="callout warn">
          <span class="label">Gotcha #1 — the one everybody hits</span>
          <p>
            Import <code>reflect-metadata</code> <b>once, at the very top of your entry
            point, before any node module loads.</b> Without it, <code>@Tool</code> metadata is
            never collected and your nodes will silently appear to have no tools at all.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <!-- ================= 2 ================= -->
      <li>
        <h3>Declare state — once, for the whole graph</h3>
        <p>
          This is the file where a LangGraph app would grow seventeen
          <code>Annotation</code> channels with hand-written reducers. Here you declare
          <em>which node owns which data</em> and the framework supplies the shape and the
          reducers.
        </p>

        <div class="code">
          <div class="code-head">
            <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
            <span class="file">weather-graph.state.ts</span>
          </div>
<pre><code>import { createGraphStateAnnotation, type NodeStateValue } from "@picoflow/ezgraph";
import { CollectNode } from "./nodes/collect.node.js";

export type WeatherGraphNodes = {
  CollectNode?: NodeStateValue&lt;{ city?: string }&gt;;
  ReportNode?: NodeStateValue&lt;{ city?: string }&gt;;
};

export const WeatherGraphState = createGraphStateAnnotation(
  CollectNode.id(),
  () =&gt; ({}) as WeatherGraphNodes,
);

export type WeatherGraphStateType = typeof WeatherGraphState.State;</code></pre>
        </div>

        <p>
          The first argument is <b>the node the conversation starts in</b>. The second supplies
          the initial per-node state object, which is where you attach your types.
        </p>

        <div class="callout">
          <span class="label">Why <code>.id()</code> and not <code>.name</code></span>
          <p>
            <code>CollectNode.id()</code> is the node's <b>durable identity</b> — the string
            that gets persisted into the session document to remember where a conversation is
            sitting. It defaults to the class name, but a node can override
            <code>static id()</code>. Always call <code>id()</code>, so an overridden identity
            keeps working.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <!-- ================= 3 ================= -->
      <li>
        <h3>Write the first node — a stage that waits</h3>
        <p>
          <code>CollectNode</code> asks for a city and waits until it gets one. This single
          class is the whole stage: its prompt, its tool schema, its handler, its per-turn
          context, and its transition decision.
        </p>

        <div class="code">
          <div class="code-head">
            <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
            <span class="file">nodes/collect.node.ts</span>
          </div>
<pre><code>import { z } from "zod";
import {
  ConversationNode,
  Tool,
  type ConversationNodeRunResult,
  type ConversationToolResult,
  type GraphNodeUpdate,
  type ToolDefinition,
} from "@picoflow/ezgraph";
import type { WeatherGraphStateType } from "../weather-graph.state.js";
import { ReportNode } from "./report.node.js";

type CollectContext = { city?: string };

export class CollectNode extends ConversationNode&lt;
  WeatherGraphStateType,
  { city?: string },  // this node's persisted local state
  CollectContext      // this turn's working context
&gt; {
  getPrompt(): string {
    return "Ask the user which city they want the weather for. When they name one, call capture_city. If they ask to stop, call terminate_session.";
  }

  defineTool(): readonly ToolDefinition&lt;{ city: string }&gt;[] {
    return [
      {
        name: "capture_city",
        description: "Record the city the user asked about.",
        schema: z.object({ city: z.string().min(1) }),
      },
    ];
  }

  @Tool("capture_city")
  captureCity(
    { city }: { city: string },
    _context: CollectContext,
  ): ConversationToolResult {
    return this.toolResult({ accepted: true })
      .withContext({ city })
      .haltAfterBatch();
  }

  protected createContext(state: WeatherGraphStateType): CollectContext {
    const saved = this.state(state).city;
    return saved === undefined ? {} : { city: saved };
  }

  protected nextStep(
    _state: WeatherGraphStateType,
    context: CollectContext,
    conversation: ConversationNodeRunResult,
  ): GraphNodeUpdate&lt;WeatherGraphStateType&gt; {
    if (conversation.quitRequested) return this.quit(conversation);
    if (context.city) {
      return this.advance(ReportNode, conversation)
        .withState({ city: context.city })
        .withStateFor(ReportNode, { city: context.city });
    }
    return this.stay(conversation);
  }
}</code></pre>
        </div>

        <p>Three things worth pausing on, because they are the design in miniature:</p>

        <ul class="checks">
          <li>
            <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M13.5 4.5L6 12 2.5 8.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
            <span><b><code>defineTool()</code> publishes; <code>@Tool</code> consumes.</b>
              Splitting them lets one node own a tool's schema while several nodes handle it.
              Notice the handler's first parameter is already a typed, parsed, Zod-validated
              object — there is no <code>try/catch</code>, and there is no
              <code>if (call.name === …)</code> dispatch, because the framework did both.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M13.5 4.5L6 12 2.5 8.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
            <span><b>The handler mutates nothing.</b> <code>withContext({ city })</code> records
              a typed <em>effect</em> that the framework applies to the turn context after the
              handler returns. <code>haltAfterBatch()</code> says "once every tool call in this
              batch has been answered, stop looping" — the batch still completes, because a
              dangling tool call is a conversation providers will reject.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M13.5 4.5L6 12 2.5 8.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
            <span><b><code>nextStep()</code> checks <code>quitRequested</code> first</b>, before
              domain success. A model can call several tools in one batch, and an explicit
              request to stop should always win over "but we also captured a city."</span>
          </li>
        </ul>

        <div class="callout warn">
          <span class="label">Gotcha #2 — <code>advance()</code> does not wait</span>
          <p>
            The single most common early misreading. <code>stay()</code> ends the turn and
            waits for the user. <code>advance()</code> routes into the target node <b>in the
            same invocation</b>, so the next stage runs immediately. If you want to set where
            the <em>next</em> user turn enters without routing there now, that's
            <code>resumeAt()</code>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <!-- ================= 4 ================= -->
      <li>
        <h3>Write the second node — a stage that finishes</h3>
        <p>
          <code>ReportNode</code> has no tools at all. It reads the city out of its own local
          state, answers, and ends the conversation. Note that its type parameters stop at two:
          the context type defaults when a node doesn't need one.
        </p>

        <div class="code">
          <div class="code-head">
            <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
            <span class="file">nodes/report.node.ts</span>
          </div>
<pre><code>import {
  ConversationNode,
  type ConversationNodeRunResult,
  type GraphNodeUpdate,
} from "@picoflow/ezgraph";
import type { WeatherGraphStateType } from "../weather-graph.state.js";

export class ReportNode extends ConversationNode&lt;
  WeatherGraphStateType,
  { city?: string }
&gt; {
  getPrompt(state: WeatherGraphStateType): string {
    const city = this.state(state).city;
    return `Report today's weather conversationally for ${city ?? "the requested city"}, then stop.`;
  }

  protected createContext(): Record&lt;string, never&gt; {
    return {};
  }

  protected nextStep(
    _state: WeatherGraphStateType,
    _context: Record&lt;string, never&gt;,
    conversation: ConversationNodeRunResult,
  ): GraphNodeUpdate&lt;WeatherGraphStateType&gt; {
    if (conversation.quitRequested) return this.quit(conversation);
    return this.finish(conversation.response ?? "All set.", conversation);
  }
}</code></pre>
        </div>

        <p>
          You have now seen the entire node contract, and it is the same contract in every
          EZGraph codebase: <code>getPrompt</code>, <code>defineTool</code>, <code>@Tool</code>
          handlers, <code>createContext</code>, <code>nextStep</code> — in that order. That
          uniformity is the point. A developer who learns it once can read a stage written by
          another team without asking anyone how <em>their</em> graph does routing.
        </p>
      </li>

      <!-- ================= 5 ================= -->
      <li>
        <h3>Assemble the graph</h3>
        <p>
          The definition declares the default model, the terminal node, and the
          <b>history spaces</b> — separate message channels so the collection stage's
          transcript never pollutes the reporting stage's context window. Then
          <code>buildGraph()</code> registers the nodes and lets routing derive itself.
        </p>

        <div class="code">
          <div class="code-head">
            <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
            <span class="file">weather-graph.ts</span>
          </div>
<pre><code>import { END } from "@langchain/langgraph";
import {
  BaseGraph,
  GRAPH_END_NODE,
  ModelCatalog,
  TerminateSessionNode,
  type GraphDefinition,
  type LlmGateway,
} from "@picoflow/ezgraph";
import { WeatherGraphState, type WeatherGraphStateType } from "./weather-graph.state.js";
import { CollectNode } from "./nodes/collect.node.js";
import { ReportNode } from "./nodes/report.node.js";

export class WeatherGraph extends BaseGraph&lt;WeatherGraphStateType&gt; {
  static getGraphDefinition(): GraphDefinition {
    return {
      llmConfig: ModelCatalog.model("openai:gpt-4o", { retries: 3 }),
      endNode: GRAPH_END_NODE,
      initialHistorySpace: "collect",
      historySpaces: [
        [CollectNode, "collect"],
        [ReportNode, "report"],
        [TerminateSessionNode, "terminal"],
      ],
    };
  }

  constructor(llmGateway: LlmGateway) {
    super(llmGateway, WeatherGraph.getGraphDefinition());
  }

  protected buildGraph() {
    const graph = this.createStateGraph(WeatherGraphState);
    graph.registerTurnNodes(CollectNode, ReportNode, TerminateSessionNode);
    graph.configAutoRoute();
    graph.addEdge(TerminateSessionNode, END);
    return graph.compile();
  }
}</code></pre>
        </div>

        <p>
          There is no edge map, no router function, and no string literal naming a node.
          <code>configAutoRoute()</code> derives the topology from what the nodes
          <em>return</em>, and every endpoint you do name is a <b>class</b> — so a typo is a
          compile error rather than a <code>KeyError</code> on a customer's third message.
        </p>

        <div class="callout warn">
          <span class="label">Gotcha #3 — <code>TerminateSessionNode</code> is not decoration</span>
          <p>
            Every <code>ConversationNode</code> subclass <em>inherits</em> a
            <code>terminate_session</code> handler, and that tool's <em>definition</em> comes
            from <code>TerminateSessionNode.defineTool()</code>. Extend
            <code>ConversationNode</code> without registering it and <code>compile()</code>
            fails with <code>Tool 'terminate_session' is not defined in this graph.</code>
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <!-- ================= 6 ================= -->
      <li>
        <h3>Run it</h3>
        <p>
          <code>GraphEngine.create()</code> reads <code>SESSION_STORE</code> from the
          environment — it defaults to <code>memory</code>, so this works with no database.
          Set your provider key and go.
        </p>

        <div class="code">
          <div class="code-head">
            <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
            <span class="file">main.ts</span>
          </div>
<pre><code>import "reflect-metadata";
import { GraphEngine } from "@picoflow/ezgraph";
import { WeatherGraph } from "./weather-graph.js";

const engine = await GraphEngine.create({ graphs: [WeatherGraph] });

// Turn 1: CollectNode has no city yet, so it stays and asks.
const first = await engine.run({
  graphName: "WeatherGraph",
  sessionId: "demo-1",
  userMessage: "hi",
});
// { status: 200, body: { success: true, completed: false, message: "Which city …" } }
// Persisted: currentNode = "CollectNode"

// Turn 2: CollectNode captures the city and advances.
const second = await engine.run({
  graphName: "WeatherGraph",
  sessionId: "demo-1",
  userMessage: "Portland",
});
// { status: 200, body: { success: true, completed: true, message: "It is 72 degrees …" } }
// Persisted: currentNode = "end", completed = true</code></pre>
        </div>

        <div class="verdict">
          <b>Between those two calls your process could restart.</b> Turn 2 reads the session
          document, sees <code>currentNode: "CollectNode"</code>, and enters that node
          directly. That resume is the entire reason the active node is persisted — and it is
          why a wait here costs you a string in a database row rather than a suspended
          coroutine you have to keep alive.
        </div>

        <p style="margin-top:22px">
          Notice what turn 2 does <em>not</em> do: it does not stop after
          <code>advance(ReportNode, …)</code>. <code>ReportNode</code> runs in the same
          invocation, produces the report, and finishes. One HTTP request, two stages.
        </p>

        <p>
          To serve this over HTTP, hand <code>run()</code> the pieces of the request and hand
          its result back — it returns <code>{ status, body, session }</code> with no HTTP
          dependency of its own, so Express, Fastify and NestJS are all thin pass-throughs.
          The <a href="/ezgraph/quote-graph/">QuoteGraph walkthrough</a> clones the real
          <a href="https://github.com/picoflowio/ezgraph-demo">ezgraph-demo</a> repo; the
          <a href="/ezgraph/#demo">demo section</a> shows the HTTP adapter in 12 lines.
        </p>
      </li>

      <!-- ================= 7 ================= -->
      <li>
        <h3>Test the conversation without a provider</h3>
        <p>
          This is the step that decides whether a guided chatbot is maintainable. You need to
          assert on multi-turn behavior in CI, deterministically, for free.
          <code>ezgraph/testing</code> is a separate entry point, so nothing in it ships in
          your runtime path.
        </p>

        <div class="code">
          <div class="code-head">
            <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
            <span class="file">test/weather-graph.spec.ts</span>
            <span class="note good">no key, no network</span>
          </div>
<pre><code>import { createTurnHarness, scriptedGateway } from "ezgraph/testing";

const harness = createTurnHarness&lt;WeatherGraphStateType&gt;({
  graph: WeatherGraph,
  gateway: scriptedGateway()
    .text("Which city would you like the weather for?")
    .callsTool("capture_city", { city: "Portland" })
    .text("It is 72 degrees in Portland."),
});

const first = await harness.send("hi");
assert.equal(first.currentNode, "CollectNode");
assert.equal(first.completed, false);

const second = await harness.send("Portland");
assert.equal(second.state?.nodes.ReportNode?.city, "Portland");
assert.equal(second.completed, true);
assert.deepEqual(second.warnings, []);

await harness.close();</code></pre>
        </div>

        <p>
          Queue the model turns in the order the graph will consume them. The three above are:
          <code>CollectNode</code>'s first answer, its tool call on turn 2, and
          <code>ReportNode</code>'s report in that same invocation.
        </p>

        <p>
          The harness drives <b>real <code>GraphEngine.run()</code> calls</b> against an
          in-memory store — so the test exercises lease, restore, invoke and persist, rather
          than calling <code>invoke()</code> directly and skipping persistence. Each turn hands
          back <code>status</code>, <code>response</code>, <code>completed</code>,
          <code>code</code>, <code>body</code>, the persisted <code>document</code>, the
          hydrated <code>state</code>, <code>currentNode</code>, <code>warnings</code> and
          <code>errors</code>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Beyond the happy path, <code>scriptedGateway()</code> can queue the failures you
          actually get paged for: <code>.empty("content_filter")</code> for a safety block,
          <code>.empty("length")</code> for a token cap, <code>.fail(err)</code> for a provider
          outage, <code>.callsTools([…])</code> for a multi-tool batch, and
          <code>.callsTool("capture_city", { city: "" })</code> to prove your schema rejection
          turns into a follow-up question instead of a 500. Inspect
          <code>gateway.calls</code> to assert on what the graph actually asked for — system
          prompt, history, tool names in publication order, model config.
        </p>
      </li>

    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================== PITFALLS ============================== -->
<section>
  <div class="wrap wrap-narrow">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Before you go further</span>
      <h2>Nine pitfalls, in the order you'll hit them</h2>
      <p>
        Straight from <a href="/ezgraph/docs/developer-guide/">§17 of the guide</a>. Reading
        this list now is cheaper than debugging any item on it later.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Pitfall</th><th>What actually happens</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><b>Storing turn data on the node</b></td>
            <td>Node instances are created once, shared by every session, and <b>frozen</b>.
              <code>this.city = …</code> throws <code>TypeError: object is not extensible</code>
              — deliberately, because that field would leak one customer's data into another's
              conversation. Per-turn data goes in the tool context; per-session data goes in
              local state via <code>withState()</code>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><b>Forgetting <code>TerminateSessionNode</code></b></td>
            <td><code>compile()</code> throws <code>Tool 'terminate_session' is not defined in
              this graph.</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><b>Checking domain success before <code>quitRequested</code></b></td>
            <td>A model can call a domain tool <em>and</em> <code>terminate_session</code> in
              one batch. Check the quit flag first or you'll ignore a user asking to stop.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><b>Assuming <code>advance()</code> waits</b></td>
            <td>It routes into the target in the same invocation. Use <code>stay()</code> to end
              the turn, or <code>resumeAt()</code> to set the next entry point without routing
              there now.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><b>Renaming a node or graph class</b></td>
            <td>The class name is the durable, persisted id. Renaming orphans live sessions
              unless you override <code>static id()</code> to return the old string. This is the
              sharpest edge in the framework.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><b>Missing <code>reflect-metadata</code></b></td>
            <td><code>@Tool</code> metadata is never collected, so nodes appear to have no
              tools — with no error to tell you why.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><b>Expecting a framework <code>expireAfter</code></b></td>
            <td>There isn't one. Implement <code>onRestoreSessionDoc()</code> and use
              <code>idleMs()</code> — that's how the benchmark app does 30-minute idle
              expiry.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><b>Reading <code>maxAgentRounds</code> as a turn limit</b></td>
            <td>It bounds <em>one node invocation's</em> model↔tool loop, not the conversation.
              Raise it for a stage that legitimately chains several tools.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><b>Narrowing <code>onEmptyModelResponse</code></b></td>
            <td>The base signature is <code>string | Promise&lt;string&gt;</code>. A
              <code>string</code>-only override fails to typecheck.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

